.TH CJSON 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cjson \- overview of the JSON parser, serializer and DOM
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cjson parses JSON text (RFC 8259) into a tree of nodes that a
program can read and change, and writes the tree back out as JSON.
Each node is reached through an opaque cjson handle.
.fi
.SH "NODE TYPES"
.nf
cjson_type(3) gives the type of a node, as a cjson_node_type_t:

  CJSON_NULL         null
  CJSON_BOOL         true or false, read with cjson_bool_val(3)
  CJSON_INTEGER      a number with no '.' and no exponent that fits a
                     long long, read with cjson_int_val(3)
  CJSON_FLOAT        all other numbers, a double, read with
                     cjson_double_val(3)
  CJSON_STRING       a UTF-8 string, read with cjson_str_val(3)
  CJSON_LIST         an array of child nodes
  CJSON_DICTIONARY   an object: string keys, each with a child node

A value accessor stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() when the
node has a different type, and for a NULL node as well, so call
cjson_type(3) before you read a node whose type comes from the input.
cjson_parse(3) gives the rules for large integers, strings and UTF-8.
.fi
.SH "PATHS"
.nf
cjson_get(3), cjson_set(3) and cjson_delete(3) find a node through a
string of components separated by dots, such as "users.#0.name".  On
an array, the component "#N" is the element at index N, where N is
one or more decimal digits with no sign, and every other component
is an object key.  Write "\e." for a dot in a key and "\e\e" for a
backslash.  cjson_set(3) creates a missing object key at the leaf,
but never a missing parent and never an array element past the end.
.fi
.SH "ORDER"
.nf
An object keeps its members in insertion order:
  - For a parsed document, the order of the text.
  - For a tree that a program makes, the order of the calls that added
    the keys.
A replaced value keeps the position of its key.  The serializers,
cjson_clone(3) and the cursor of cjson_dictionary_first(3) all use
this order, so one tree gives the same output in every run.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP"
.nf
cjson_parse(3), cjson_clone(3) and the cjson_create_*(3) functions give
a tree that the caller owns and must free with cjson_destroy(3).

cjson_get(3), cjson_list_get(3), cjson_dictionary_get(3) and the
dictionary cursor give borrowed references that the tree keeps
owning.  Clone such a node first if you want to attach it somewhere
else.

cjson_list_push(3) and cjson_dictionary_set(3) take the child, and
only ccol_invalid_args leaves it with the caller.

Each node records its allocator, so destroying a tree needs no
allocator from the caller.  A string from cjson_serialize(3) is freed
with the root's allocator.
.fi
.SH "LIMITS"
.nf
A parse refuses nesting deeper than 500 containers, and so do the
serializers and cjson_clone(3) for a tree that a program builds.
The size of a parsed tree is linear in the length of the input, and
cjson_parse(3) gives the bound.  A string never holds a null byte,
because each one is a null-terminated C string.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A tree is not thread safe, but many threads can read one tree at the
same time as long as no thread changes it.  Separate trees are
independent, and separate threads can parse and serialize at once.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_parse (3),
.BR cjson_serialize (3),
.BR cjson_get (3),
.BR cjson_set (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cjson_list_push (3),
.BR cjson_destroy (3),
.BR cyaml (7)
